<p>A few general-purpose reminders from the world of computing.</p>
<h3>Static sites</h3>
<p>A static site is just files: HTML, CSS, JavaScript and assets. With no server-side code to run, hosting is cheap, fast and hard to break. Build once, serve anywhere.</p>
<h3>Build pipelines</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep sources and generated output separate.</li>
<li>Make builds repeatable: pin versions and lock dependencies.</li>
<li>Automate the boring parts: compile, minify, deploy.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section>
<h2>🖥 Shell Basics <code>html</code></h2>
<p>Handy commands worth knowing by heart:</p>
<pre><code>ls -la          # list files, including hidden
grep -rn foo .  # search recursively
git log --oneline
curl -I https://example.com
node --version</code></pre>
<h3>Versioning</h3>
<p>Semantic versioning uses <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>: breaking changes, new features, then fixes.</p>
